CONTROL-LAW-CEILING-2026-09-10.md:46:C1 was reconstructed independently over all 48 realised boundaries. Every anchor matched the recorded committed bits and joules within `2e-11` relative tolerance and matched served/target counts exactly.
CONTROL-LAW-CEILING-2026-09-10.md:48:## C2 method and optimality status
CONTROL-LAW-CEILING-2026-09-10.md:50:For each realised boundary and TDM slot, C2 chooses OFF or a non-dominated DVB-S2 ACM mode for every transmitting beam. A link decoded by C1 may only retain or increase its credited mode; a failed C1 transmission may be switched off or assigned any frontier mode. This pointwise non-regression makes every C1-served user's integrated bits nondecreasing, so the unchanged service guard and rate target cannot regress; the reported endpoint counts verify that invariant.
CONTROL-LAW-CEILING-2026-09-10.md:54:The outer search uses Dinkelbach ratio iterations. At each price it performs deterministic coordinate ascent from two starts/orders over the non-dominated mode frontier. This is bounded and reproducible, but it is not exhaustive over joint discrete modes. Therefore **C2 is best-known, not a certified optimum or ceiling**. The exact inner solve certifies only the minimum RF powers for each visited feasible mode vector.
CONTROL-LAW-CEILING-2026-09-10.md:58:The decomposition is exact and additive. For C1 efficiency `eta1`, each link-slot receives its rate change minus `eta1` times its allocated power change; dividing the conserved total by C2 joules reproduces `eta2-eta1`. PA and circuit power belong to their transmitting link; satellite baseband power is divided equally among that satellite's active slot links. Buckets use the C1 geometry/state, so the classification is not selected after seeing C2.
